Python 3 port
=============

This source now builds on CPython 3.10 or newer with setuptools and an installed
compatible EB library/header set. The distribution remains ebmodule; the native
import is eb, matching eblib and the C initializer. Python 2 is no longer a build
target. This is a legacy single-phase extension, not a promise of isolated
subinterpreter or free-threaded support.

The low-level API returns bytes for EB-encoded text, titles, alternative glyph
text, image data and raw buffers. Search queries, keyword tuples, text writes
and bitmap inputs also take bytes. Encode using the dictionary's encoding
(EUC-JP for the supplied examples); the extension never implicitly decodes EB
bytes as UTF-8. Filesystem bind paths take str. Error message APIs return bytes.

The eblib convenience layer accepts str search/write arguments and encodes them
as EUC-JP; content results remain bytes. Decode after joining complete chunks,
not independently per chunk, because a multibyte character may cross a boundary.
The examples decode explicitly and preserve source characters without ignore
error handlers. Binary/image output remains bytes, including embedded NULs.

Set EB_LIBRARY_PREFIX to an owned EB installation prefix to select its include
and lib directories together. Build with python3 -m pip install . in an isolated
environment; configure the platform runtime loader for that same lib directory.
No system-wide installation is needed. The checked-in EB 3.1 library is supported:
text_status() returns its native content-mode code, and eb_is_text_stopped() reads
its text-end flag. eb_forward_text(book, appendix) uses EB 3.x default native hooks
(the old C API takes a hookset); on EB 4.x it passes the appendix. Constants absent
from older headers, such as decoration hooks and EB_ERR_END_OF_CONTENT, are omitted.
Run:

    python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v

For the real dictionary gate, set EB_TEST_FIXTURE_ROOT to the fixtures directory
of the retained eb4j packet (commit bccd2f8b852928a42076e7af54675c7240544364),
keeping its license notices, and run tests/test_real_epwing.py through unittest.
It checks pinned source hashes, plain/compressed search, Japanese text, hook
exceptions/recovery and chunk boundaries, raw bytes, BMP binary reads, real gaiji
font data/conversions, and appendix binding. Without the variable those tests
explicitly skip; that is an incomplete real-fixture gate. No data is bundled here.

The retained books have exactword/word indexes; endword/keyword calls verify the
actual unsupported-index errors. Successful endword/keyword search still requires
a legitimate dictionary containing those indexes. The binary test reads a bounded
HONMON slice through the native monochrome BMP API; it does not claim an authored
picture, WAVE, or JPEG fixture. Those media paths remain unqualified.

Callbacks retain the original Python exception and traceback. Hook batches are
validated before registration, and replaced callbacks/cycles can be collected.
Recursive text reading from callbacks is rejected before entering EB's shared
native cache; callbacks may write replacement bytes with eb_write_text APIs.
Windows compilation/import remains a separate required gate when Windows binaries
are distributed. No system-wide installation is required by these instructions.

Python 3 migration intent credited to rroessler1 (aehlke/ebmodule#1). The completed
port retains binary data as bytes and uses named modern PyTypeObject fields,
PyType_Ready and nonfatal checked module initialization rather than the raw PR's
positional ABI edits and blanket UTF-8 Unicode conversions.
